    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nCourt Bans BYD From Selling Its Land Rover Defender Copycat in Europe\n8 photos\nPhoto: tthg 04 | TikTok\nA district court in The Hague banned Chinese automaker BYD from selling its Ti7 SUV in the European Union, ruling that the vehicle infringes on EU design rights. It is happening just a couple of months after BYD's European offices were raided following Jaguar Land Rover’s claims that the Chinese company copied its Defender's design and technology.\nThe district court in The Hague issued the preliminary injunction banning BYD from selling its Fangchengbao Ti7 in Europe. The preliminary relief judge determined that the seven-seat Ti7 copies \" characteristic and distinctive elements of the Defender. \"\nThe report highlighted the resemblence between the two , especially focusing on flat-roof silhouette and boxy appearance of the Chinese SUV, along with the upright proportions and tailgate styling, squared-off wheel arches, and the solid, body-colored panel behind the rear passenger door.\nThe injunction goes into effect one week after the ruling on November 20, 2026. Beyond banning imports, sales, and advertising across all 27 member states of the European Union, the court also barred BYD from producing the Ti7 at its upcoming plant in Szeged, Hungary, which is slated to begin mass production in early 2027.\nPhoto: JLR The restriction remains in force until the full intellectual property dispute is resolved. If BYD violates the EU ban, it faces daily fines of €100,000 or €25,000 per vehicle ($27,500), capped at €10 million ($11 million), Reuters reports .\nThe Dutch court ruled that it does not have jurisdiction over BYD’s affiliate in the United Kingdom. JLR originally initiated the lawsuit earlier in 2026, after BYD listed the Ti7 for order on its UK website, with a price starting at 47,995 pounds ($63,010), which undercuts the Defender by a solid 20,000 pounds ($26,260).\nCustomer deliveries in the UK are planned to start in January 2027 and are by no means affected by the decision. JLR is expected to file a separate UK design-infringement lawsuit in British courts before those January deliveries begin. In the meantime, a separate lawsuit filed by JLR in Rotterdam, alleging copyright infringement and \" slavish imitation ,\" will proceed independently.\nBYD stated that it \"strongly disagrees\" with the decision, claiming that the Ti7 SUV is the result of independent design work and confirming it will appeal the ruling. JLR noted that the legal proceedings remain ongoing.\nPhoto: BYD Back in August, court-appointed bailiffs, acting for JLR, seized digital files from BYD’s European offices in Schiedam and Hoofddorp, Netherlands. BYD argued that the seizure was a \" fishing expedition \" that swept up unrelated data.\nOn November 12, the District Court of The Hague ordered Jaguar Land Rover to destroy or return documents and digital records seized during the August raid on BYD’s Dutch offices. JLR was ordered to destroy the materials within 24 hours and provide third-party confirmation to BYD. Non-compliance carried a penalty of 50,000 euros ($54,840) per hour, capped at a total of 500,000 euros ($584,400).\nJLR has a mixed track record in intellectual property court battles over vehicle styling, with court appearances starting long before the BYD Ti7 was in the making. Back in 2016, after JLR ended production of the classic Defender , billionaire Sir Jim Ratcliffe, now also a shareholder of the Mercedes-AMG Petronas Formula 1 Team, alongside Mercedes-Benz and Toto Wolff, offered to buy the tooling and rights to continue to build it.\nJLR refused, so Ratcliffe founded INEOS Automotive to build what he saw as a spiritual successor to the original Defender, the INEOS Grenadier . JLR attempted to block the launch of the Grenadier by registering the 3D exterior design of the classic Defender as a trademark in the United Kingdom, but INEOS opposed the applications.\nPhoto: JLR Both the UK Intellectual Property Office and the High Court ruled against JLR. The court found that the classic Defender’s boxy silhouette was not distinctive enough to function as a trademark, noting that the differences from the utilitarian 4x4s were only noticeable to specialists, not average buyers.\nThe decision eventually cleared the way for the Grenadier’s production, though JLR did successfully secure the 3D shape trademarks for the classic Defender in the United States. However, the INEOS Grenadier is also marketed in North America.\nThis happened two years after Chinese automaker Jiangling unveiled the Landwind X7, an SUV closely resembling the Range Rover, marketing it at roughly a third of the price of the British model. The Chinese had actually filed a design patent for the X7 even before JLR patented the Evoque in China, leading to years of patent invalidation fights.\nJLR lost the design patent and copyright claims and sued Jiangling Motor Corporation, along with its joint venture subsidiary Jiangling Motor Holding, as well as an authorized Beijing dealership that sold the Landwind X7.\nPhoto: BYD In 2019, a Beijing court ruled in JLR's favor. The court found that Jiangling copied five distinct exterior features of the Range Rover Evoque, causing consumer confusion, and ordered an immediate halt to sales. Jiangling was also ordered to pay 1.5 million Chinese yuan (171,861 pounds or $224,840) in damages to the British automaker.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nJaguar Land Rover JLR BYD BYD Ti7 ineos\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT Rolls-Royce Phantom Is a CGI Steroid Overdose. You've Never Seen It This Muscular, Have You?",
